## Essential Vec Utility Methods

Beyond `push` and `pop`, Vec provides several utility methods for common operations.

### len — Size

```xiom
let v = Vec[Int]();
v.push(10); v.push(20); v.push(30);
let size = v.len();  // 3
```

### is_empty — Check for Content

```xiom
let v = Vec[Int]();
let empty = v.is_empty();  // true
v.push(1);
let has_items = v.is_empty();  // false
```

### clear — Remove All Elements

```xiom
var v = Vec[Int]();
v.push(1); v.push(2); v.push(3);
v.clear();
// v.len() == 0, v.is_empty() == true
```

### Practical Patterns

```xiom
fn process_queue(queue: &mut Vec[Str]) -> Vec[Str] {
  var results = Vec[Str]();
  while !queue.is_empty() {
    match queue.pop() {
      Some(item) => { results.push("Processed: " + item); }
      None => { }
    }
  }
  return results;
}
```

### Guard Clauses with len

```xiom
fn first_element(v: &Vec[Int]) -> Option[Int] {
  if v.is_empty() {
    return None;
  }
  return v.get(0);
}

fn last_element(v: &Vec[Int]) -> Option[Int] {
  if v.len() == 0 {
    return None;
  }
  return v.get(v.len() - 1);
}
```

### Combining Methods

```xiom
fn extend(v: &mut Vec[Int], other: &Vec[Int]) -> Unit {
  var i = 0;
  while i < other.len() {
    v.push(other[i]);
    i = i + 1;
  }
}
```

### Method Summary

| Method | Returns | Description |
|--------|---------|-------------|
| `.len()` | Int | Number of elements |
| `.is_empty()` | Bool | True if no elements |
| `.push(item)` | Unit | Add to end |
| `.pop()` | Option[T] | Remove from end |
| `.get(i)` | Option[T] | Safe indexed access |
| `.clear()` | Unit | Remove all elements |

